</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-11-20.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="035107"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#035107" label="[03:51:07]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">rekado: I think it is best for you to maintain control over that domain (I don't think any one person should control too much bootstrapping bits; that way if anything goes sideways damage can be routed around)</span></div><div class="line" id="035229"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#035229" label="[03:52:29]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unmush: thank you for exploring the C# bootstrap chain and discovering its problems; patching tends to be the easiest option long term (until a replacement can be made or obtained)</span></div><div class="line" id="035441"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#035441" label="[03:54:41]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jackdk: my work around was to just disconnect from the network and change the clock on the system. Do the build; restore the clock and then resume the rest of the bootstrap. (it was annoying and isn't viable on remote systems for various reasons) but yeah substitutes remain a source of problem attitudes towards bootstrapping.</span></div><br /></div></body></html>